
# The cPanel-generated error_log/log_errors block that used to sit here named
# /home/tw123457/logs/php.error.log outright. One repo now deploys two cPanel
# accounts (issue #182), and .htaccess has no way to parameterise a path, so a
# literal here is wrong on one host by construction — it pointed the new
# xiangshuicn.cc site's PHP error log at a directory that does not exist there.
#
# Dropped rather than parameterised, because this layer never needed to set it
# at all: cPanel generates a php.ini and a .user.ini in each account's own
# document root, and both already carry that account's correct error_log path
# (verified on the new host: /home/xiangshu/logs/php.error.log, log_errors On).
# Those files are per-account and stay on the server, so PHP keeps logging to
# the right place on both sites with nothing here to repeat it. Do not
# reintroduce an error_log directive in any .htaccess this repo ships — it can
# only ever name one of the two accounts.
#
# Note this file holds PHP directives only. The site's URL routing lives one
# level up, in the account home directory's .htaccess — see
# .remote-home-htaccess, which the deploy uploads there (issue #201).

# Added: the reverse-proxy handler (index.php) forwards the raw request
# body via php://input to the Node process. PHP auto-parses
# multipart/form-data and x-www-form-urlencoded bodies into $_POST/$_FILES,
# which consumes php://input and leaves it empty -- breaking every
# multipart upload (e.g. listing photos) forwarded through the proxy.
<IfModule php8_module>
   php_flag enable_post_data_reading off
</IfModule>
<IfModule lsapi_module>
   php_flag enable_post_data_reading off
</IfModule>
